15. Common mistakes and JEE traps

Explain each of the following:

* Assuming acceleration is zero at the highest point. Correct: acceleration remains vertically downward with magnitude g.
* Assuming the whole velocity becomes zero at the highest point. Only the vertical component becomes zero.
* Confusing speed with a velocity component.
* Using degrees and radians inconsistently.
* Forgetting the sign of vertical displacement when upward is positive.
* Using T = 2u sin θ/g when launch and landing heights differ.
* Using R = u² sin 2θ/g without checking the equal-height assumption.
* Forgetting that time is shared by horizontal and vertical motion.
* Assuming equal ranges mean equal flight times or maximum heights.
* Treating a vector’s component magnitudes as signed components.
* Rounding intermediate values too early.
* Forgetting units in numerical answers.

