SECTION 2 — WORK DONE BY A CONSTANT FORCE

Work describes energy transferred by a force when an object undergoes displacement.

For a constant force F and displacement s:

W = Fs cos θ

where θ is the angle between force and displacement.

Vector form:

W = F · s

Explain the dot product and why work is a scalar.

SI unit: joule (J).

1 J = 1 N m

Dimensional formula:

[W] = [ML²T⁻²]

Types of work

Positive work: force has a component in the direction of displacement.

Negative work: force has a component opposite to displacement.

Zero work: force is perpendicular to displacement, or displacement is zero.

Explain that the work done by a force depends on the force and the displacement, not merely on whether the object is moving.

Examples:

* Gravity does positive work on an object falling downward.
* Gravity does negative work on an object moving upward.
* Normal reaction does zero work for motion along a fixed horizontal surface when the normal is perpendicular to the displacement.
* Centripetal force does zero instantaneous work in ideal uniform circular motion because it is perpendicular to the instantaneous velocity.

Warn that a force perpendicular to displacement does zero work only for the displacement being considered.

Worked example 1

A force of 10 N acts on an object that moves 5 m. The angle between the force and displacement is 60°.

W = Fs cos θ

W = 10 × 5 × cos 60° = 25 J.

Worked example 2

A 20 N force acts perpendicular to a displacement of 3 m.

W = 20 × 3 × cos 90° = 0 J.

Explain why a nonzero force can do zero work.

SECTION 3 — WORK DONE BY MULTIPLE FORCES

The net work done is the sum of the work done by each force:

W_net = W₁ + W₂ + W₃ + …

For constant mass, the work-energy theorem will connect net work to the change in kinetic energy.

Teach students to identify the work done by:

* Applied force
* Gravity
* Friction
* Normal reaction
* Tension
* Spring force

Explain that forces must be considered separately before summing their work.

Worked example 3

A 4 kg block moves 5 m horizontally. A horizontal force of 12 N acts forward, while friction of 4 N acts backward. Other forces do zero work.

Work by applied force:

W_applied = 12 × 5 = 60 J

Work by friction:

W_friction = −4 × 5 = −20 J

Net work:

W_net = 60 − 20 = 40 J.

SECTION 4 — WORK DONE BY A VARIABLE FORCE

When force changes with position, the work is calculated by integration.

In one dimension:

W = ∫[x₁ to x₂] Fₓ(x) dx

Explain that the signed area under the force-versus-position graph gives work.

* Area above the position axis contributes positive work when the positive direction is chosen consistently.
* Area below the axis contributes negative work.
* For a constant force, the graph is a rectangle.
* For a linearly varying force, the graph may form a triangle or trapezium.

Worked example 4

A force varies with position according to Fₓ = 2x, where F is in newtons and x is in metres. Find the work from x = 0 to x = 3 m.

W = ∫₀³ 2x dx

W = [x²]₀³ = 9 J.

Include a labelled Fₓ–x graph and explain how its area matches the integral.

SECTION 5 — WORK-ENERGY THEOREM

State the theorem:

The net work done on a particle equals the change in its kinetic energy.

W_net = ΔK = K_f − K_i

Kinetic energy:

K = ½mv²

Therefore:

W_net = ½mv² − ½mu²

For constant mass:

W_net = ½m(v² − u²)

Give an intuitive explanation: positive net work increases kinetic energy; negative net work decreases it.

Clarify:

* Use net work, not the work done by only one force unless it is the only force doing work.
* The theorem applies to the change in kinetic energy of a particle or suitable system.
* The theorem does not require constant acceleration.
* For variable forces, calculate work using integration or a graph where necessary.

Worked example 5

A 2 kg object speeds up from 3 m/s to 7 m/s. Find the net work done.

W_net = ½m(v² − u²)

W_net = ½ × 2 × (49 − 9) = 40 J.

Worked example 6

A 5 kg block moving at 10 m/s experiences a constant opposing net force of 10 N. Find the stopping distance.

Initial kinetic energy = ½ × 5 × 10² = 250 J.

At stopping, final kinetic energy = 0.

Net work = −250 J.

Since the net force is opposite displacement:

−10s = −250

s = 25 m.

Explain why the negative work equals the loss of kinetic energy.

SECTION 6 — KINETIC ENERGY

Kinetic energy is the energy associated with motion:

K = ½mv²

Properties:

* It is a scalar.
* It is non-negative in classical mechanics.
* It depends on mass and speed.
* Doubling speed makes kinetic energy four times as large.
* Doubling mass doubles kinetic energy at the same speed.

SI unit: joule.

Dimensional formula:

[ML²T⁻²]

Explain that kinetic energy depends on the reference frame because measured speed depends on the reference frame.

SECTION 7 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY

Near Earth’s surface, the change in gravitational potential energy is:

ΔU = mgΔh

If height is measured upward from a chosen reference level:

U = mgh + constant

The usual choice U = mgh sets the potential energy to zero at h = 0.

Explain that the zero of potential energy is arbitrary, but differences in potential energy are physically meaningful.

Work done by gravity:

W_gravity = −ΔU

For a fall through vertical height h:

W_gravity = +mgh

For an upward displacement through height h:

W_gravity = −mgh.

Worked example 7

A 2 kg object rises vertically by 5 m. Take g = 10 m/s².

ΔU = mgΔh = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J.

Work done by gravity = −100 J.

Explain why the potential-energy increase is positive while the work done by gravity is negative.

SECTION 8 — CONSERVATIVE AND NON-CONSERVATIVE FORCES

A conservative force has work that depends only on the initial and final positions, not the path taken between them.

Equivalent closed-path property:

∮ F · dr = 0

For a conservative force:

W_conservative = −ΔU

Examples:

* Gravity
* Ideal spring force

Friction is a common example of a non-conservative force in introductory mechanics because its work depends on the path travelled.

Explain that non-conservative forces can transfer mechanical energy into thermal energy or other forms.

Do not state that total energy disappears due to friction. Explain that mechanical energy can decrease while total energy is conserved when all relevant energy forms are included.

SECTION 9 — CONSERVATION OF MECHANICAL ENERGY

Mechanical energy:

E_mechanical = K + U

If only conservative forces do work, or if the net work by non-conservative forces is zero:

K_i + U_i = K_f + U_f

Explain how kinetic and potential energy transform into each other.

Worked example 8 — Falling object

An object is released from rest at height 20 m. Ignore air resistance and take g = 10 m/s². Find its speed just before reaching the ground.

Initial energy:

E_i = mg(20)

Final energy:

E_f = ½mv²

Equating:

mg(20) = ½mv²

v = √(2 × 10 × 20) = 20 m/s.

Explain why the mass cancels.

Worked example 9 — Vertical projection

An object is projected vertically upward at speed u. Neglect air resistance.

At the highest point, v = 0.

Using conservation of energy:

½mu² = mgH

Therefore:

H = u²/(2g).

Explain the equivalence with the constant-acceleration kinematics result.

SECTION 10 — SPRING FORCE AND SPRING POTENTIAL ENERGY

For an ideal spring obeying Hooke’s law:

F_spring = −kx

where:

* k is the spring constant.
* x is displacement from the natural-length equilibrium position.
* The negative sign means the spring force points toward equilibrium.

SI unit of k: N/m.

Dimensional formula:

[k] = [MT⁻²]

Spring potential energy:

U_s = ½kx²

Explain that the spring energy is measured relative to the natural-length position in the ideal spring model.

Work done by the spring as it moves from x₁ to x₂:

W_spring = ½kx₁² − ½kx₂²

Work done by an external agent in a slow, quasi-static change from x₁ to x₂, when kinetic energy does not change:

W_external = ½k(x₂² − x₁²)

State the conditions behind this last expression.

Worked example 10

A spring with k = 200 N/m is compressed by 0.1 m from its natural length.

U_s = ½ × 200 × (0.1)² = 1 J.

Explain why compression and extension by equal magnitudes store equal energy in the ideal spring model.

SECTION 11 — BLOCK AND SPRING SYSTEMS

Teach the method:

1. Choose the initial and final states.
2. Identify kinetic energy.
3. Identify gravitational potential energy.
4. Identify spring potential energy.
5. Determine the work done by non-conservative forces.
6. Apply energy conservation or the work-energy theorem.
7. Check whether the result satisfies physical constraints.

For an ideal horizontal block-spring system with no friction:

½mv² + ½kx² = constant.

At the equilibrium position x = 0, spring potential energy is zero relative to that position and kinetic energy is maximum for a given oscillation amplitude.

At the turning points, speed is zero and spring potential energy is maximum.

Clarify that this does not mean the spring force is zero at the turning points.

Worked example 11

A 1 kg block attached to an ideal spring of k = 100 N/m is released from rest at x = 0.2 m from equilibrium on a frictionless horizontal surface.

Initial energy:

E = ½ × 100 × (0.2)² = 2 J.

At equilibrium, x = 0:

½mv² = 2

v = 2 m/s.

Verify the result using the given values.

SECTION 12 — WORK DONE BY FRICTION

For a constant kinetic-friction force of magnitude f_k acting opposite displacement s:

W_friction = −f_k s

For horizontal sliding under the standard model:

f_k = μ_k N.

If there are no other vertical forces and no vertical acceleration:

N = mg.

Thus:

W_friction = −μ_k mgs.

Explain that this expression cannot be applied blindly when the normal force differs from mg or when the friction magnitude changes.

Mechanical-energy accounting:

W_nonconservative = Δ(K + U)

For friction alone:

W_friction = Δ(K + U)

Explain the sign convention and how friction reduces mechanical energy in the usual sliding scenario.

Worked example 12

A 2 kg block slides 4 m on a horizontal surface with μ_k = 0.25. Take g = 10 m/s². Assume no other vertical forces and constant kinetic friction.

N = mg = 20 N.

f_k = 0.25 × 20 = 5 N.

W_friction = −5 × 4 = −20 J.

Explain that the block’s mechanical energy decreases by 20 J due to friction, with the energy transferred to other forms.

SECTION 13 — POWER

Power is the rate at which work is done or energy is transferred.

Average power:

P_avg = W/Δt

Instantaneous power:

P = dW/dt

For a force acting on a moving object:

P = F · v

Therefore:

P = Fv cos θ

where θ is the angle between force and velocity.

If force and velocity point in the same direction:

P = Fv.

If force is opposite velocity, the power of that force is negative.

SI unit: watt (W).

1 W = 1 J/s.

Dimensional formula:

[P] = [ML²T⁻³]

Explain that power measures the rate of energy transfer, not the total amount of energy transferred.

Worked example 13

A motor exerts a constant force of 100 N on an object moving at 5 m/s in the same direction.

P = Fv = 100 × 5 = 500 W.

Worked example 14

A 1000 kg car travels at constant speed 20 m/s on a level road. The total resistive force is 500 N.

At constant speed, the driving force balances the resistive force.

Required driving force = 500 N.

Power required to balance the resistance:

P = Fv = 500 × 20 = 10,000 W = 10 kW.

Explain that this is the mechanical power against the stated resistance, not necessarily the motor’s input power.

SECTION 14 — VARIABLE FORCE AND INSTANTANEOUS POWER

If a force varies with time or position, use:

P = F · v

when instantaneous force and velocity are known.

If power is known as a function of time:

P = dW/dt

Therefore:

W = ∫P dt.

Explain that power can vary even if the applied force remains constant, because speed can change.

Include one worked example involving a known power function and calculate the work transferred over a specified interval using integration.

SECTION 15 — EFFICIENCY

Efficiency is the ratio of useful output energy or power to input energy or power:

η = useful output/input

As a percentage:

η% = (useful output/input) × 100%.

For steady operation where input and output powers are measured over the same interval:

η = P_out/P_in.

Explain that for a real device, efficiency is at most 100% under ordinary energy-accounting conventions, with some input energy transferred into unwanted forms.

Worked example 15

A motor receives 1000 W of input power and supplies 800 W of useful mechanical output power.

η = 800/1000 = 0.8 = 80%.

Useful output power = 800 W.

SECTION 16 — ENERGY METHOD VERSUS NEWTON’S LAWS

Explain when energy methods are useful:

* Finding speed at a position.
* Finding maximum height.
* Solving spring compression or extension.
* Handling variable forces through work integrals.
* Comparing initial and final states without finding the full time history.

Explain when Newton’s laws are often more direct:

* Finding acceleration at a particular instant.
* Finding tension or normal reaction.
* Solving force constraints in connected bodies.

Clarify that energy conservation alone may not determine every unknown force or time.

