Explain each concept from the beginning and state the assumptions behind every formula.

SECTION 2 — NEWTON’S UNIVERSAL LAW OF GRAVITATION

Every pair of point masses attracts one another with a force directed along the line joining their centres.

The magnitude is:

F = Gm₁m₂/r²

where:

* m₁ and m₂ are the masses.
* r is the separation between the point masses, or between the centres of spherically symmetric bodies when the external-field result applies.
* G is the universal gravitational constant.

Use:

G ≈ 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg².

Dimensional formula:

[G] = [M⁻¹L³T⁻²].

Explain:

* Gravitational force is attractive.
* Doubling one mass doubles the force.
* Doubling the separation reduces the force to one-quarter.
* The force between the two bodies has equal magnitude and opposite directions.

For vector notation, define the direction carefully. If r₁ and r₂ are position vectors of masses m₁ and m₂, respectively, show the force on m₁ due to m₂ as:

F₁₂ = Gm₁m₂(r₂ − r₁)/|r₂ − r₁|³.

Explain that this vector points from m₁ toward m₂.

Worked example 1

Two masses of 5 kg and 10 kg are separated by 2 m in empty space. Calculate the gravitational force.

F = G(5)(10)/2²

F = 12.5G ≈ 8.34 × 10⁻¹⁰ N.

Verify the units and powers of ten.

SECTION 3 — SUPERPOSITION OF GRAVITATIONAL FORCES

The net gravitational force on a mass is the vector sum of the forces exerted by all other masses.

F_net = ΣFᵢ.

Explain why forces must be added as vectors rather than by adding magnitudes blindly.

For forces along one line, choose a positive direction and use signed values.

For perpendicular forces, use components.

Worked example 2

A test mass experiences gravitational forces of 6 N east and 8 N north due to two other bodies.

Net force magnitude:

F_net = √(6² + 8²) = 10 N.

Direction:

θ = tan⁻¹(8/6) measured north of east.

Explain that the force directions are determined by the positions of the attracting bodies.

SECTION 4 — GRAVITATIONAL FIELD

The gravitational field at a point is gravitational force per unit test mass:

g_vec = F_vec/m.

For a point mass M at distance r:

g = GM/r².

The field vector points toward the mass.

SI unit:

N/kg, equivalent to m/s².

Dimensional formula:

[g] = [LT⁻²].

For a spherical body outside its surface, the field is the same as if the body’s mass were concentrated at its centre, provided the body has the appropriate spherical symmetry.

Explain the distinction:

* G is universal and constant.
* g depends on the source mass, distance, and environment.

Worked example 3

Calculate g at a distance of 2R from the centre of a spherical planet whose surface gravitational acceleration is g₀ and whose radius is R.

Outside the planet:

g(r) = GM/r².

At the surface:

g₀ = GM/R².

At r = 2R:

g = GM/(2R)² = g₀/4.

SECTION 5 — ACCELERATION DUE TO GRAVITY AT EARTH’S SURFACE

For a spherical Earth of mass M and radius R:

g = GM/R².

Use g ≈ 9.8 m/s² near Earth’s surface, or g = 10 m/s² when explicitly permitted as an approximation.

Explain that the numerical value varies slightly with location because Earth rotates, is not a perfect sphere, and has variations in its mass distribution.

The weight of an object in a local gravitational field is approximately:

W = mg.

Clarify that weight is a gravitational force, whereas mass is a property of the object.

Worked example 4

A 4 kg object is at a location where g = 9.8 m/s².

W = mg = 4 × 9.8 = 39.2 N.

SECTION 6 — VARIATION OF g WITH ALTITUDE

For a spherical Earth, at altitude h above the surface:

g_h = GM/(R + h)².

Using g₀ = GM/R²:

g_h = g₀[R/(R + h)]².

For h much smaller than R, use the first-order approximation:

g_h ≈ g₀(1 − 2h/R).

Explain that this approximation is valid only when h/R is small.

Worked example 5

At an altitude equal to Earth’s radius, h = R.

g_h = g₀[R/(R + R)]²

g_h = g₀/4.

Explain why the distance from Earth’s centre is 2R, not R.

SECTION 7 — VARIATION OF g WITH DEPTH

For a uniform-density spherical Earth, at depth d below the surface:

r = R − d.

Inside a uniform-density sphere, only the mass enclosed within radius r contributes to the gravitational field; the gravitational contributions of exterior spherical shells cancel.

For uniform density:

M_r = M(r³/R³).

Therefore:

g_r = GM_r/r² = GMr/R³.

Using g₀ = GM/R²:

g_d = g₀r/R.

Thus:

g_d = g₀(1 − d/R).

State clearly that this linear formula assumes a uniform-density spherical Earth. Earth’s real density varies with depth.

At the centre:

g = 0.

Explain why gravitational field is zero at the centre of an ideal uniform sphere by symmetry.

Worked example 6

At a depth d = R/4, calculate g under the uniform-density approximation.

g_d = g₀(1 − 1/4) = 3g₀/4.

SECTION 8 — EFFECT OF EARTH’S ROTATION

Explain that an object’s apparent weight can differ from the gravitational force because the object participates in Earth’s rotation.

For an ideal spherical Earth rotating with angular speed ω, at latitude λ, the distance to Earth’s rotation axis is R cos λ.

The centrifugal acceleration magnitude is:

a_cf = ω²R cos λ.

Its direction is away from the rotation axis, so its component opposing the radial gravitational direction is:

ω²R cos² λ.

Under the simplified spherical-Earth model, the radial component of effective gravity is approximately:

g_eff,radial = GM/R² − ω²R cos² λ.

Explain that this is the radial component in the simplified model, not the complete vector expression for effective gravity at every latitude.

At the equator, λ = 0°:

g_eff,radial = g − ω²R.

At the poles, λ = 90°:

the centrifugal contribution has no radial component.

State that the actual Earth is oblate and has a nonuniform mass distribution, so this is an idealized result.

SECTION 9 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL

Gravitational potential at a point is gravitational potential energy per unit mass, with the usual zero chosen at infinity.

For a point mass M:

V = −GM/r.

SI unit:

J/kg.

Dimensional formula:

[V] = [L²T⁻²].

Explain why the potential is negative under this reference convention: work must be supplied by an external agent to move a test mass slowly from a finite distance to infinity against attraction.

For several point masses:

V_total = Σ(−GMᵢ/rᵢ).

Potential is a scalar, so potentials are added algebraically rather than as vectors.

SECTION 10 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY

For two point masses with potential energy zero at infinite separation:

U = −Gm₁m₂/r.

For a small test mass m in the potential of a source mass M:

U = mV = −GMm/r.

Explain:

* Potential is energy per unit mass.
* Potential energy depends on the test mass.
* The negative sign comes from the chosen zero at infinity.
* Changes in potential energy are physically meaningful.

Work done by gravity:

W_gravity = −ΔU.

For moving slowly between two positions, work done by an external agent is:

W_external = ΔU,

provided the initial and final kinetic energies are equal and the only relevant energy transfer is through the specified gravitational interaction.

Worked example 7

A 2 kg mass is at distance r from a point mass M. Write its gravitational potential energy using the zero-at-infinity convention.

U = −2GM/r.

Explain the units and the negative sign.

SECTION 11 — RELATION BETWEEN FIELD AND POTENTIAL

For radial gravitational motion:

g_r = −dV/dr

where the signed radial component is positive outward.

Since:

V = −GM/r,

differentiate:

dV/dr = GM/r².

Therefore:

g_r = −GM/r².

The negative sign indicates that the field points inward.

Explain that gravitational field points in the direction of decreasing potential.

Do not confuse the positive magnitude GM/r² with the signed radial component.

SECTION 12 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL OF A SPHERICAL SHELL

Teach the standard results for an ideal thin spherical shell of mass M and radius R.

Outside the shell, r > R:

V = −GM/r

g = GM/r² in magnitude, directed inward.

On the surface, r = R:

V = −GM/R

g = GM/R² in magnitude, directed inward just outside the shell.

Inside the shell, r < R:

g = 0

V = −GM/R, constant throughout the interior.

Explain that the interior potential is not zero merely because the interior field is zero.

The potential remains constant because its spatial derivative is zero inside the shell, and its value matches the surface potential continuously.

Include a clearly labelled graph or diagram of field magnitude and potential versus distance for a thin shell. Distinguish the field graph from the potential graph.

SECTION 13 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL OF A UNIFORM SOLID SPHERE

For a uniform-density solid sphere of mass M and radius R:

Outside, r ≥ R:

V = −GM/r

g = GM/r² in magnitude.

Inside, r < R:

Enclosed mass:

M_r = Mr³/R³.

Field magnitude:

g(r) = GMr/R³.

Potential, using zero at infinity:

V(r) = −GM(3R² − r²)/(2R³).

At the surface:

V(R) = −GM/R.

At the centre:

V(0) = −3GM/(2R).

Explain that the potential is most negative at the centre while the field magnitude is zero there.

Make clear that the interior formulas assume uniform density.

SECTION 14 — GRAVITATIONAL POTENTIAL ENERGY AND ESCAPE

Explain escape as reaching arbitrarily large distance with zero final speed in the ideal limiting case, without further propulsion or energy loss.

For a mass m initially at radius R from a spherical planet of mass M, with initial speed v_e:

Initial energy:

E_i = ½mv_e² − GMm/R.

For the limiting escape trajectory, final energy at infinity is zero:

E_f = 0.

Set E_i = E_f:

½mv_e² − GMm/R = 0.

Therefore:

v_e = √(2GM/R).

Using g = GM/R²:

v_e = √(2gR).

Explain the assumptions:

* The planet is treated as spherical and nonrotating for the basic result.
* Air resistance and other bodies’ gravity are neglected.
* The escaping object is not propelled after launch.
* Escape speed is independent of the escaping object’s mass.

Worked example 8

A planet has surface gravity g and radius R. Find the escape speed from its surface.

v_e = √(2gR).

Explain why surface gravity alone is insufficient to give a numerical speed unless R or equivalent planetary information is known.

SECTION 15 — ORBITAL SPEED

For a satellite of mass m in a circular orbit of radius r around a spherical planet of mass M:

Gravity provides the required centripetal force:

GMm/r² = mv²/r.

Cancel m:

v_orbit = √(GM/r).

Explain that the orbit radius r is measured from the planet’s centre, not from its surface.

At altitude h:

r = R + h.

Therefore:

v_orbit = √[GM/(R + h)].

Using g = GM/R² at the surface:

v_orbit = √[gR²/(R + h)].

For a circular orbit at the surface-radius limit:

v_orbit = √(gR).

State that an actual satellite cannot orbit inside a planet’s solid surface.

Worked example 9

A satellite moves in a circular orbit of radius 4R around a planet. Given the planet’s surface gravitational acceleration g₀ and radius R, find the orbital speed.

v = √[GM/(4R)].

Since GM = g₀R²:

v = ½√(g₀R).

SECTION 16 — TIME PERIOD OF A CIRCULAR ORBIT

For a circular orbit:

T = 2πr/v.

Substitute v = √(GM/r):

T = 2π√(r³/GM).

Therefore:

T² = 4π²r³/(GM).

Explain that a larger circular orbit has a longer orbital period.

For altitude h above a planet’s surface:

T = 2π√[(R + h)³/(GM)].

Clearly state that these formulas describe ideal circular orbits.

SECTION 17 — ENERGY OF A SATELLITE IN A CIRCULAR ORBIT

For a satellite of mass m in a circular orbit of radius r:

K = ½mv².

Using v² = GM/r:

K = GMm/(2r).

Gravitational potential energy:

U = −GMm/r.

Total mechanical energy:

E = K + U = −GMm/(2r).

Thus:

K = −E

U = 2E.

Explain the signs carefully:

* K is positive.
* U is negative.
* Total energy is negative for a bound circular orbit.

Explain that these relations apply to an ideal circular orbit, not every possible trajectory.

Worked example 10

A satellite of mass m orbits a planet of mass M in a circular orbit of radius r. Find its total mechanical energy.

E = −GMm/(2r).

Explain why a negative total energy indicates a bound orbit under the chosen zero-at-infinity convention.

SECTION 18 — CHANGE OF ORBIT

Teach the energy method for moving a satellite between two circular orbits.

Initial circular-orbit energy:

E_i = −GMm/(2r_i).

Final circular-orbit energy:

E_f = −GMm/(2r_f).

Change in mechanical energy:

ΔE = E_f − E_i

ΔE = GMm/2(1/r_i − 1/r_f).

Explain the sign:

* Moving to a higher circular orbit increases total mechanical energy, so ΔE is positive.
* Moving to a lower circular orbit decreases total mechanical energy, so ΔE is negative.

Explain that an actual orbit-transfer maneuver involves dynamics and may require multiple burns. Do not equate the net orbital-energy change with the work of gravity alone.

SECTION 19 — KEPLER’S LAWS

First law: Law of orbits

Planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one focus.

Explain that a circle is a special case of an ellipse.

Second law: Law of areas

The line joining a planet and the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times.

Explain its relation to conservation of angular momentum under a central force.

For a central force, torque about the force centre is zero, so angular momentum is conserved.

Third law: Law of periods

For bodies orbiting the same central mass:

T² ∝ a³,

where a is the semimajor axis of the orbit.

For an ideal two-body orbit with central mass M and negligible orbiting mass compared with M:

T² = 4π²a³/(GM).

For circular orbits, a = r.

Explain that the same proportionality constant applies to orbits around the same central body under the stated approximation.

Worked example 11

Two satellites orbit the same planet in circular orbits of radii r and 4r. Their periods are T₁ and T₂.

T₂/T₁ = (4r/r)^(3/2) = 8.

The outer satellite’s period is eight times the inner satellite’s period.

SECTION 20 — WEIGHTLESSNESS AND APPARENT WEIGHT

Explain that astronauts in orbit experience apparent weightlessness because the spacecraft and occupants are in continuous free fall together.

Gravity is still present in a low Earth orbit.

For a body in a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal acceleration.

Clarify:

* Weightlessness does not mean gravitational field is zero.
* A scale reads normal contact force, not the gravitational field directly.
* Apparent weight is zero in ideal free fall when there is no supporting contact force.

Compare an orbiting satellite with an elevator in free fall, stating the idealizations.

