SECTION 2 — ELASTICITY AND PLASTICITY

When an external force deforms a solid, its dimensions or shape may change.

Elastic deformation: the body returns approximately to its original shape when the deforming force is removed, provided it remains within its elastic range.

Plastic deformation: some deformation remains after the force is removed.

Explain that real materials have limits, and ideal elastic models are approximations.

Introduce:

* Original length L
* Change in length ΔL
* Original cross-sectional area A
* Applied force F

Use a labelled diagram of a wire before and after stretching.

SECTION 3 — STRESS

Stress is the internal restoring force per unit area in a material under deformation.

For a uniformly loaded cross-section in a simple axial problem:

Stress = F/A

SI unit: pascal (Pa).

1 Pa = 1 N/m².

Dimensional formula:

[Stress] = [ML⁻¹T⁻²].

Types of stress

Longitudinal stress: force per unit area acting normally to a cross-section and producing extension or compression.

Volumetric stress: pressure applied to change the volume of a body.

Shear stress: tangential force per unit area that tends to change the shape of a body.

Explain that stress is not the same as the external force: stress depends on the area over which the force acts.

Worked example 1

A wire with cross-sectional area (2 \times 10^{-6},\text{m}^2) is pulled by a force of 100 N.

Stress:

[
\sigma=\frac{F}{A}
=\frac{100}{2\times10^{-6}}
=5\times10^7\ \text{Pa}.
]

Check the conversion of area into square metres.

SECTION 4 — STRAIN

Strain describes relative deformation and is dimensionless.

Longitudinal strain

[
\text{Longitudinal strain}=\frac{\Delta L}{L}.
]

For extension, ΔL is positive under the usual convention. For shortening, it is negative.

Volumetric strain

[
\text{Volumetric strain}=\frac{\Delta V}{V}.
]

Shear strain

For a small angular deformation, shear strain is approximately the change in angle in radians:

[
\gamma\approx\tan\theta\approx\theta
]

when θ is small.

Explain the distinction between deformation (which has dimensions) and strain (which is a ratio).

Worked example 2

A 2 m wire extends by 1 mm.

Convert:

[
\Delta L=1\text{ mm}=10^{-3}\text{ m}.
]

Then:

[
\text{Strain}=\frac{10^{-3}}{2}=5\times10^{-4}.
]

SECTION 5 — HOOKE’S LAW

Within the proportional or linear-elastic range, stress is proportional to strain:

[
\text{Stress}\propto\text{Strain}.
]

For a wire undergoing small longitudinal deformation:

[
\text{Stress}=Y(\text{Longitudinal strain}),
]

where Y is Young’s modulus.

Explain that Hooke’s law applies only within the appropriate range; it is not a universal rule for arbitrarily large deformation.

Distinguish:

* Proportional limit
* Elastic limit
* Yielding
* Plastic deformation
* Fracture

Explain that these points may differ and that not every material displays a sharply defined yield point.

SECTION 6 — YOUNG’S MODULUS

Young’s modulus measures resistance to longitudinal deformation in the linear-elastic regime.

[
Y=\frac{\text{Longitudinal stress}}
{\text{Longitudinal strain}}.
]

For a wire of original length L, cross-sectional area A, and extension ΔL under axial force F:

[
Y=\frac{F/A}{\Delta L/L}.
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{\Delta L=\frac{FL}{AY}}.
]

Also:

[
F=\frac{AY}{L}\Delta L.
]

SI unit: Pa.

Dimensional formula:

[
[Y]=[ML^{-1}T^{-2}].
]

Explain:

* A larger Young’s modulus means less strain for the same stress in the linear-elastic regime.
* Extension increases with applied force and original length.
* Extension decreases with cross-sectional area and Young’s modulus.

Worked example 3

A wire has:

* L = 2 m
* A = (1\times10^{-6},\text{m}^2)
* Y = (2\times10^{11},\text{Pa})
* F = 100 N

Calculate its extension.

[
\Delta L=\frac{FL}{AY}
=\frac{100\times2}
{(10^{-6})(2\times10^{11})}
=10^{-3}\text{ m}.
]

Therefore, the extension is 1 mm.

Verify the units and arithmetic.

SECTION 7 — BULK MODULUS

Bulk modulus measures resistance to uniform volume change under pressure.

Using a sign convention in which compressive pressure increase is positive:

[
B=-\frac{\Delta P}{\Delta V/V}.
]

Equivalently:

[
B=-\frac{\Delta P,V}{\Delta V}.
]

For ordinary compression, ΔP is positive and ΔV is negative, making B positive.

SI unit: Pa.

Explain that a larger bulk modulus means a smaller fractional volume change for the same small pressure change.

Compressibility is the reciprocal of bulk modulus:

[
\kappa=\frac{1}{B}.
]

SI unit of compressibility: Pa⁻¹.

State that these relations describe the appropriate small-deformation material response.

Worked example 4

A solid has bulk modulus (1\times10^{10},\text{Pa}). A pressure increase of (2\times10^6,\text{Pa}) is applied.

Calculate the fractional volume change under the small-deformation model.

[
\frac{\Delta V}{V}\approx-\frac{\Delta P}{B}
=-\frac{2\times10^6}{10^{10}}
=-2\times10^{-4}.
]

Explain the negative sign as a decrease in volume.

SECTION 8 — SHEAR MODULUS

Shear modulus measures resistance to shear deformation.

[
G_s=\frac{\text{Shear stress}}{\text{Shear strain}}.
]

Use (G_s) for shear modulus in this chapter to avoid confusing it with the universal gravitational constant G.

For a small angular deformation:

[
G_s=\frac{F/A}{\gamma}.
]

For a rectangular block of height L undergoing a small lateral displacement x:

[
\gamma\approx\frac{x}{L}.
]

Therefore, in the linear-elastic regime:

[
G_s=\frac{FL}{Ax}.
]

Explain that the block’s shape changes, while the small-strain model assumes the material remains in its elastic range.

Worked example 5

A block with:

* A = 0.02 m²
* L = 0.1 m
* F = 100 N
* x = 1 mm

Find the shear modulus.

Convert (x=10^{-3},\text{m}).

[
G_s=\frac{FL}{Ax}
=\frac{100(0.1)}{0.02(10^{-3})}
=5\times10^5\ \text{Pa}.
]

SECTION 9 — POISSON’S RATIO

When a wire or rod is stretched longitudinally, its lateral dimensions usually decrease.

Poisson’s ratio is defined in the small-strain regime as:

[
\nu=-\frac{\text{Lateral strain}}
{\text{Longitudinal strain}}.
]

The minus sign makes ν positive for ordinary materials that contract laterally when stretched longitudinally.

Explain:

* Longitudinal strain describes deformation along the applied load.
* Lateral strain describes deformation perpendicular to the applied load.
* Poisson’s ratio is dimensionless.
* Its value depends on the material and the material model.

Do not state a universal range that applies to every possible material. If discussing common isotropic, linearly elastic materials, explicitly state those assumptions.

Worked example 6

A wire has longitudinal strain (2\times10^{-3}) and lateral strain (-6\times10^{-4}).

[
\nu=-\frac{-6\times10^{-4}}{2\times10^{-3}}=0.3.
]

SECTION 10 — RELATION BETWEEN ELASTIC MODULI

For a homogeneous, isotropic, linearly elastic material:

[
Y=2G_s(1+\nu).
]

Also:

[
Y=3B(1-2\nu).
]

Combining these gives:

[
Y=\frac{9BG_s}{3B+G_s}.
]

Explain that these relations rely on the homogeneous, isotropic, linear-elastic material model. Do not apply them automatically to anisotropic or nonlinear materials.

Worked example 7

For an appropriate isotropic material, (G_s=4\times10^{10},\text{Pa}) and (\nu=0.25).

[
Y=2G_s(1+\nu)
=2(4\times10^{10})(1.25)
=10^{11},\text{Pa}.
]

SECTION 11 — STRETCHING A WIRE: IMPORTANT DEPENDENCIES

From:

[
\Delta L=\frac{FL}{AY},
]

teach the following for a uniform wire in the linear-elastic regime:

* Doubling F doubles the extension.
* Doubling L doubles the extension.
* Doubling A halves the extension.
* Doubling Y halves the extension.

For a circular cross-section of diameter d:

[
A=\frac{\pi d^2}{4}.
]

Therefore:

[
\Delta L=\frac{4FL}{\pi d^2Y}.
]

Explain that extension varies inversely with the square of diameter when all other quantities are held constant.

Worked example 8

Two wires have the same material, length, and applied force. Wire B has twice the diameter of wire A.

Since area is proportional to diameter squared:

[
A_B=4A_A.
]

Therefore:

[
\Delta L_B=\frac14\Delta L_A.
]

SECTION 12 — WIRES IN SERIES AND PARALLEL

Teach the assumptions first: uniform wires, linear-elastic behaviour, small deformations, and ideal connections.

Wires in series

For two wires joined end-to-end under the same tensile force F:

[
\Delta L_{\text{total}}=\Delta L_1+\Delta L_2.
]

For each wire:

[
\Delta L_i=\frac{FL_i}{A_iY_i}.
]

Therefore:

[
\Delta L_{\text{total}}
=F\left(\frac{L_1}{A_1Y_1}
+\frac{L_2}{A_2Y_2}\right).
]

Explain that the same tensile force acts through ideal series-connected wires, while the extensions add.

Wires in parallel

For two wires connecting the same rigid supports, their extensions are equal:

[
\Delta L_1=\Delta L_2.
]

The total load is the sum of the loads carried by the wires:

[
F=F_1+F_2.
]

Use:

[
F_i=\frac{A_iY_i}{L_i}\Delta L.
]

Explain that the load division depends on each wire’s axial stiffness (A_iY_i/L_i).

Include a worked numerical example for series wires and another for parallel wires. State all dimensions and material properties.

SECTION 13 — ELASTIC POTENTIAL ENERGY

When a wire is stretched gradually within the linear-elastic regime, the applied force rises from zero to F as the extension increases from zero to ΔL.

The stored elastic energy equals the work done by the external force for a quasistatic loading process with no energy loss:

[
U=\frac12F\Delta L.
]

For a uniform wire:

[
U=\frac{F^2L}{2AY}.
]

Using (F=AY\Delta L/L):

[
U=\frac{AY(\Delta L)^2}{2L}.
]

Explain that the factor 1/2 arises because force increases linearly with extension from zero to its final value.

Worked example 9

A wire is stretched by 2 mm under a final force of 50 N, assuming linear-elastic behaviour and quasistatic loading.

[
U=\frac12F\Delta L
=\frac12(50)(0.002)=0.05\text{ J}.
]

SECTION 14 — ELASTIC ENERGY DENSITY

Elastic energy density is stored elastic energy per unit volume.

[
u=\frac{U}{V}.
]

For a linearly elastic material under uniaxial stress:

[
u=\frac12(\text{stress})(\text{strain}).
]

Therefore:

[
u=\frac12Y(\text{strain})^2.
]

Also:

[
u=\frac{\text{stress}^2}{2Y}.
]

These forms assume linear-elastic uniaxial deformation.

SI unit: J/m³, equivalent to Pa.

Worked example 10

A material has Young’s modulus (2\times10^{11},\text{Pa}) and longitudinal strain (10^{-3}).

[
u=\frac12Y\epsilon^2
=\frac12(2\times10^{11})(10^{-3})^2
=10^5\ \text{J/m}^3.
]

SECTION 15 — STRESS-STRAIN GRAPH

Create a labelled, readable stress-strain graph for a representative ductile material. Clearly state that exact curve shapes and transition points vary by material.

Explain:

* Initial linear-elastic region.
* Proportional limit.
* Elastic limit.
* Yielding or yield region where applicable.
* Plastic deformation.
* Ultimate tensile stress.
* Fracture point.

Explain that the slope of the initial linear region of a uniaxial stress-strain curve is Young’s modulus.

The area under a stress-strain curve represents work done per unit volume under the appropriate loading assumptions. Up to the relevant point, it corresponds to stored elastic energy density for reversible linear-elastic loading. Beyond elastic behaviour, some work may be dissipated and not recoverable.

Do not suggest every material has a clearly defined yield plateau or the same stress-strain curve.

SECTION 16 — STRENGTH, ELASTICITY, AND SAFETY

Distinguish:

* Stiffness: resistance to deformation, related to elastic modulus.
* Strength: stress a material can withstand before a specified failure or yielding criterion.
* Toughness: energy absorbed per unit volume before fracture, represented by the appropriate total area under the stress-strain curve for the test.
* Elasticity: ability to recover deformation when the load is removed within the relevant elastic range.

Explain why a material can be stiff but brittle, or less stiff but capable of substantial plastic deformation.

Introduce a factor of safety at a conceptual level:

[
\text{Factor of safety}
=\frac{\text{specified failure strength}}
{\text{allowable working stress}},
]

when the chosen definition and strength criterion match.

Explain that engineering design requires material-specific data and appropriate safety standards; a single textbook formula cannot replace them.

