SECTION 2 — WAVE EQUATION AND WAVE SPEED

Introduce the fundamental relationship:

[
v=f\lambda
]

Also:

[
v=\frac{\lambda}{T}
]

Define every symbol and SI unit.

Explain:

* Wave speed is the speed at which a particular phase travels.
* Particle speed is the instantaneous speed of a medium particle.
* These two speeds are not generally equal.
* Frequency is set by the source and remains unchanged when a wave enters another medium, provided the source frequency stays fixed.
* Wavelength changes when wave speed changes.

For a sinusoidal progressive wave travelling in the positive (x)-direction:

[
y(x,t)=A\sin(kx-\omega t+\phi)
]

For a wave travelling in the negative (x)-direction:

[
y(x,t)=A\sin(kx+\omega t+\phi)
]

where:

[
k=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda},\qquad \omega=2\pi f
]

Explain the phase constant (\phi), wave number (k), and angular frequency (\omega).

Use consistent sign conventions and explain how the sign before (\omega t) identifies the propagation direction for the stated phase form.

Include examples that determine amplitude, wavelength, frequency, wave speed, and propagation direction from a given wave equation.

SECTION 3 — PARTICLE VELOCITY AND ACCELERATION

For the wave:

[
y(x,t)=A\sin(kx-\omega t+\phi)
]

derive the particle’s transverse velocity:

[
v_y=\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
=-A\omega\cos(kx-\omega t+\phi)
]

and acceleration:

[
a_y=\frac{\partial^2y}{\partial t^2}
=-\omega^2y
]

Explain that these are the transverse velocity and acceleration of a medium particle for this sinusoidal wave, not the propagation speed of the wave.

Include:

* Maximum particle speed (A\omega).
* Maximum acceleration magnitude (A\omega^2).
* The distinction between wave speed and particle speed.
* A worked numerical example.

SECTION 4 — PHASE AND PHASE DIFFERENCE

